 **Note:**  The **GNN** model only works with genes (for example CpG methylation sites does not work). The reason is that GNNs require a prior knowledge network, which is currently set to use STRING database.
 
+.. class:: infomark
+
+**Note:**  When using the **GNN** model, four graph convolution types are available:
+
+- **GC** (GraphConv — default): applies separate learned linear transformations to the node itself and to its neighbors, then aggregates neighbors by mean. A good general-purpose baseline.
+- **GCN** (GCNConv): aggregates neighbor features using a symmetrically normalized mean, where weights are determined by node degrees. Computationally efficient and treats all neighbors equally weighted by their connectivity.
+- **SAGE** (SAGEConv): aggregates neighbors by mean with a learnable transformation, then concatenates with the node's own features. Unlike GCN it is inductive — it can generalize to nodes not seen during training.
+
 -----
 
